#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""huddle: let coding agents (Claude Code, Codex) talk to each other and start each other as helpers.

Two ways in:
- An agent starts a helper: `huddle spawn codex "review PR 12"` starts a Codex agent, gives it the task, and
  prints its reply. `huddle send NAME "..."` continues with the same agent. Works from Claude or Codex, either way.
- Two threads the user already has open connect with /huddle (Claude Code) and $huddle (Codex).

Each agent is identified by its own thread (CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID / CODEX_THREAD_ID), and a helper by
HUDDLE_MEMBER. Anyone else, such as you in a terminal, is "you".

Delivery: a helper is driven by a runner process (`huddle _run`) that speaks the app's own protocol (Codex:
`codex app-server`; Claude Code: `claude -p --input-format stream-json`), so every message reaches it as a user
turn and its final message is its reply. Codex threads the user opened are woken with `codex queue`; Claude Code
wakes when its background `huddle wait` exits.

No loops by construction: a message an agent sends is a request and gets exactly one reply. A reply never
makes a new request. If a reply reaches a helper between tasks, the helper's answer goes up to the agent that
started it, and from there to the user.

State lives in one SQLite file (~/.huddle/huddle.db, override with HUDDLE_HOME). No server, no port.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 error, 2 choose a huddle, 3 no reply yet, 4 huddle ended.

https://github.com/sourabhrathourr/huddle
"""

